If you are looking for **existing off-the-shelf apps** (for mobile, desktop, or web) that automatically plot your photos on a satellite/terrain map with a connected route line and photo popups just like your web app, several dedicated options handle this out of the box:

### 1. Travel & Hike Storytelling Apps (Closest Match to Your Screenshot)

* **Polarsteps (iOS, Android, Web):** Automatically tracks your route with a dotted red path on a satellite/terrain map, pins your photos along the exact trail coordinates, and generates a shareable web link for friends and family to view without installing anything.
* **Relive (iOS, Android):** Built specifically for hikes, bike rides, and mountain trips. It takes your photos and GPS track (or photo timestamps) and turns them into a 3D flyover video and interactive map over satellite terrain.
* **AllTrails (iOS, Android, Web):** If you record your hike with AllTrails (or upload a GPX/photo set to a completed activity), it plots all your geotagged photos directly along the trail line on a 3D satellite map.

### 2. Photo Map & EXIF Desktop/Mobile Apps

* **PhotoMap (Android) / PhotoMapper & GeoTag (iOS):** Dedicated mobile apps that scan your camera roll's EXIF data and plot every photo on a satellite map with clustering, timeline playback, and KMZ/GPX export.
* **Adobe Lightroom Classic (Map Module — Windows/Mac):** Displays your photo filmstrip on the left and a full satellite/terrain map in the center, automatically pinning photos by GPS coordinates with clickable photo previews.
* **DigiKam (Windows, Mac, Linux — Free & Open Source):** Includes a built-in **Map View** powered by OpenStreetMap/satellite tiles that groups geotagged photos on a map alongside a thumbnail sidebar.

### 3. Self-Hosted / Photo Library Alternatives

* **Immich (Web, iOS, Android — Free & Open Source):** A self-hosted Google Photos alternative that includes a full-screen interactive **Map View** showing heatmaps and clickable photo pins across all your uploaded photos.
* **Google Earth (Web & Pro):** As you saw earlier, importing the `.kmz` file generated by your own app (`Download .KMZ` in your screenshot) gives you the exact same sidebar + pinned photo popups + 3D terrain inside Google Earth.



